  "data": {
    "description": "Compute the kBET (k-nearest-neighbor Batch Effect Test) statistic for an embedding Z given batch labels. For each cell, the function finds the k nearest neighbors in embedding space (using sklearn NearestNeighbors), counts the batch composition in that neighborhood and compares it to the global batch proportions using a chi-square goodness-of-fit test (degrees of freedom = n_batches - 1). Neighborhoods where the null hypothesis (neighborhood follows global batch proportions) is rejected at alpha=0.05 are counted as rejections. The function returns the kBET rejection rate: fraction of neighborhoods rejected (value in [0,1]; higher indicates stronger remaining batch effect). The implementation documents recommended k choices (e.g. between ~1% and 5% of total cells) and handles edge cases: if a neighborhood has too-small expected counts for a chi-square approximation, the neighborhood is skipped and not counted in the denominator; if all neighborhoods are skipped the function returns NaN. Input assumptions: Z is (n_cells × n_features) numpy array; batch_labels is 1D array-like with categorical labels (will be converted to integer categories). The function is pure-NumPy/Scikit-learn and returns a single float rejection rate.",
    "path": null,
    "module_id": null,
    "is_async": false,
    "calls": [],
    "params": [
      {
